# Learn Rust Series (#42) - Drop Order, the Drop Check & Leak Safety

![rust-banner.png](https://images.hive.blog/DQmRy55NqSCCyvRhSxKbu3rDFGp3MEtNMcMVTyync7TWuBC/rust-banner.png)

### What will I learn
- You will learn the exact order values and struct fields are dropped in;
- what the **drop check** is, and why a type with a `Drop` impl constrains the lifetimes it holds;
- the difference between a leak and a use-after-free, and why Rust permits the former but not the latter;
- how `std::mem::forget`, `Box::leak`, and `ManuallyDrop` let you skip or defer destruction on purpose;
- what Rust's \

. Those are different promises, and the gap between them is exactly where `std::mem::forget`, `Box::leak` and `ManuallyDrop` live. By the end you will see why safe code is allowed to leak on purpose, and why that is not the hole in the type system it first appears to be ;-)

## Solutions to Episode 41 Exercises

Last episode we built `MyRc`, a stripped-down reference-counted pointer, from a raw heap allocation. There were three exercises, and here is full, runnable code for each.

**Exercise 1** asked you to add a `strong_count(this: &MyRc<T>) -> usize` _associated function_ (called as `MyRc::strong_count(&a)`, not as a method) matching the signature style of the real `Rc::strong_count`:

```rust
use std::cell::Cell;
use std::ops::Deref;
use std::ptr::NonNull;

struct RcBox<T> {
    count: Cell<usize>,
    value: T,
}

struct MyRc<T> {
    ptr: NonNull<RcBox<T>>,
}

impl<T> MyRc<T> {
    fn new(value: T) -> MyRc<T> {
        let boxed = Box::new(RcBox { count: Cell::new(1), value });
        MyRc { ptr: NonNull::new(Box::into_raw(boxed)).unwrap() }
    }
    fn count(&self) -> usize {
        unsafe { self.ptr.as_ref().count.get() }
    }
    // The exercise: an ASSOCIATED function, so `this` is spelled out explicitly.
    fn strong_count(this: &MyRc<T>) -> usize {
        // SAFETY: `this` is a live handle, so its allocation is alive.
        unsafe { this.ptr.as_ref().count.get() }
    }
}

impl<T> Clone for MyRc<T> {
    fn clone(&self) -> MyRc<T> {
        unsafe {
            let c = self.ptr.as_ref().count.get();
            self.ptr.as_ref().count.set(c + 1);
        }
        MyRc { ptr: self.ptr }
    }
}

impl<T> Deref for MyRc<T> {
    type Target = T;
    fn deref(&self) -> &T {
        unsafe { &self.ptr.as_ref().value }
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for MyRc<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        unsafe {
            let c = self.ptr.as_ref().count.get();
            if c == 1 {
                drop(Box::from_raw(self.ptr.as_ptr()));
            } else {
                self.ptr.as_ref().count.set(c - 1);
            }
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let a = MyRc::new(String::from(\

, MyRc::strong_count(&a)); // 1
}
```

The whole point of the exercise was the _shape_ of the API, not the body. `Rc` exposes `strong_count` as an associated function taking `&self` spelled out as `this: &Rc<T>`, precisely so that `.strong_count()` cannot collide with a method of the same name on the wrapped type -- remember from episode 19 that `Deref` would happily forward a `.strong_count()` call through to the inner value if such a method existed. Writing it as `MyRc::strong_count(&a)` sidesteps that ambiguity entirely. The body just reads the shared counter, same as `.count()` does, which is why both print `2`.

**Exercise 2** wanted a `ptr_eq(&self, other: &MyRc<T>) -> bool` reporting whether two handles point at the _same_ allocation, by comparing raw pointers:

```rust
use std::cell::Cell;
use std::ptr::NonNull;

struct RcBox<T> {
    count: Cell<usize>,
    value: T,
}

struct MyRc<T> {
    ptr: NonNull<RcBox<T>>,
}

impl<T> MyRc<T> {
    fn new(value: T) -> MyRc<T> {
        MyRc { ptr: NonNull::new(Box::into_raw(Box::new(RcBox { count: Cell::new(1), value }))).unwrap() }
    }
    fn ptr_eq(&self, other: &MyRc<T>) -> bool {
        // Two handles are \

 when they address the same RcBox.
        self.ptr.as_ptr() == other.ptr.as_ptr()
    }
}

impl<T> Clone for MyRc<T> {
    fn clone(&self) -> MyRc<T> {
        unsafe {
            let c = self.ptr.as_ref().count.get();
            self.ptr.as_ref().count.set(c + 1);
        }
        MyRc { ptr: self.ptr }
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for MyRc<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        unsafe {
            let c = self.ptr.as_ref().count.get();
            if c == 1 { drop(Box::from_raw(self.ptr.as_ptr())); }
            else { self.ptr.as_ref().count.set(c - 1); }
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let a = MyRc::new(42);
    let b = a.clone();      // same allocation as a
    let c = MyRc::new(42);  // equal VALUE, different allocation
    println!(\

, a.ptr_eq(&c)); // false
}
```

Notice that no `unsafe` is needed here at all: reading `NonNull::as_ptr` and comparing two raw pointers is a perfectly safe operation, because we never _dereference_ them. `ptr_eq` deliberately compares _identity_, not _value_ -- `a` and `c` both wrap `42`, but they live in different allocations, so `ptr_eq` reports `false`. This is the same distinction `Rc::ptr_eq` draws, and it matters when you use reference counting to model shared _identity_ (two names for one thing) rather than mere equality.

**Exercise 3** was the deliberate cliff-hanger: a `try_unwrap(self) -> Result<T, MyRc<T>>` returning `Ok(value)` when the count is exactly 1 and `Err(self)` otherwise. The hint said to reach for `std::mem::forget` -- and it is the perfect bridge into today's topic, so read this one closely:

```rust
use std::alloc::{dealloc, Layout};
use std::cell::Cell;
use std::ptr::NonNull;

struct RcBox<T> {
    count: Cell<usize>,
    value: T,
}

struct MyRc<T> {
    ptr: NonNull<RcBox<T>>,
}

impl<T> MyRc<T> {
    fn new(value: T) -> MyRc<T> {
        MyRc { ptr: NonNull::new(Box::into_raw(Box::new(RcBox { count: Cell::new(1), value }))).unwrap() }
    }
    fn count(&self) -> usize {
        unsafe { self.ptr.as_ref().count.get() }
    }
    fn try_unwrap(self) -> Result<T, MyRc<T>> {
        if self.count() != 1 {
            return Err(self); // someone else still holds a handle
        }
        unsafe {
            // We are the sole owner. Move the value OUT of the allocation without
            // dropping it -- we are about to hand it back to the caller.
            let value = std::ptr::read(&self.ptr.as_ref().value);
            let raw = self.ptr.as_ptr();
            // Stop our own Drop from running: it would try to free the box AGAIN.
            std::mem::forget(self);
            // Now free just the RcBox allocation by hand. The value was already
            // moved out, and Cell<usize> has no destructor, so nothing double-drops.
            dealloc(raw as *mut u8, Layout::new::<RcBox<T>>());
            Ok(value)
        }
    }
}

impl<T> Clone for MyRc<T> {
    fn clone(&self) -> MyRc<T> {
        unsafe {
            let c = self.ptr.as_ref().count.get();
            self.ptr.as_ref().count.set(c + 1);
        }
        MyRc { ptr: self.ptr }
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for MyRc<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        unsafe {
            let c = self.ptr.as_ref().count.get();
            if c == 1 { drop(Box::from_raw(self.ptr.as_ptr())); }
            else { self.ptr.as_ref().count.set(c - 1); }
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
