## Leaks are safe; use-after-free is not

Now the philosophical heart of the episode. Rust guarantees **memory safety**: no use-after-free, no double-free, no reading uninitialised memory. It does _not_ guarantee that every destructor runs. Leaking memory -- holding an allocation forever and never freeing it -- is considered perfectly **safe**, because a leak, however wasteful, cannot corrupt anything or hand you a dangling pointer. The worst a leak does is waste memory. A use-after-free, by contrast, is undefined behaviour that can crash or be exploited. Rust draws its hard line between those two, not where you might first expect.

The clearest demonstration is `Box::leak`, which takes an owned heap allocation and hands you back a plain reference with a `'static` lifetime -- a reference that lives for the rest of the program, because the box behind it is never freed:

```rust
fn main() {
    let boxed = Box::new(42);
    let leaked: &'static mut i32 = Box::leak(boxed); // deliberately never freed
    *leaked += 1;
    println!(\

); // 43 -- this memory lives for the rest of the run
}
```

That is a leak, in broad daylight, in entirely safe code -- no `unsafe` block in sight. And it is genuinely useful: `Box::leak` is a common way to initialise some global configuration or interned string once at startup and get a `'static` reference you can hand out everywhere without lifetime gymnastics. You are trading a fixed chunk of memory for the whole program run in exchange for a lifetime that never expires.

Its cousin `std::mem::forget` goes the other way: it consumes a value and simply _skips_ its destructor, without freeing anything the value owns. This is also safe, for the same reason -- failing to clean up cannot cause unsoundness:

```rust
use std::mem;

struct Noisy;
impl Drop for Noisy {
    fn drop(&mut self) { println!(\

); }
}

fn main() {
    let n = Noisy;
    mem::forget(n);          // destructor skipped: nothing prints for `n`
    println!(\

);
}
```

Run it and you see only `forgot it`. `Noisy`'s destructor never fires, because `forget` told the compiler to walk away from the value without running its cleanup. In everyday code you rarely want this on its own -- an accidental `forget` is just a memory leak. But paired with a manual free, as we did in `try_unwrap` above, it becomes the precise tool for moving a value out of a place without letting that place's destructor run a second time.

## ManuallyDrop: taking control of when

Sometimes you need finer control than \

. You need to decide the _exact instant_ a value's destructor fires -- typically inside `unsafe` code that is shuffling a value's guts around. `ManuallyDrop<T>` is the tool: it wraps a value, suppresses the automatic drop entirely, and lets you invoke the destructor yourself, once, when you are ready:

```rust
use std::mem::ManuallyDrop;

struct Resource;
impl Drop for Resource {
    fn drop(&mut self) { println!(\

); }
}

fn main() {
    let mut r = ManuallyDrop::new(Resource);
    println!(\

 prints exactly here
    // no second drop happens at the closing brace -- ManuallyDrop saw to that
}
```

The message prints at the `ManuallyDrop::drop` call, not at the closing brace, and it prints _once_. And because you hold the trigger, you can override the normal ordering rules entirely -- here two resources are released in the _opposite_ order from their declaration, which rule 1 would never give you by itself:

```rust
use std::mem::ManuallyDrop;

struct Res(&'static str);
impl Drop for Res {
    fn drop(&mut self) { println!(\

, self.0); }
}

fn main() {
    let mut a = ManuallyDrop::new(Res(\

));
    // Normally A (declared first) would drop LAST. Here WE choose the order:
    unsafe {
        ManuallyDrop::drop(&mut a); // releasing A -- first
        ManuallyDrop::drop(&mut b); // releasing B -- second
    }
}
```

`ManuallyDrop::drop` is `unsafe` for a very good reason: calling it twice, or touching the value after you have dropped it, is a use-after-free. That is why it is a specialist tool -- you meet it in the internals of smart pointers, collections, and anything doing raw memory surgery, but almost never in ordinary application code. The takeaway is not that you will write this every day; it is that Rust's safety guarantee is narrow and precise on purpose. It promises you will never touch freed memory, and in exchange it accepts that you might, occasionally and deliberately, leak. That trade is exactly why safe code is allowed escape hatches like `forget` and `leak` at all -- they cannot break the one promise that actually matters.

